// Runtime/BufferOwnership.maxon — ⭐⭐ **ONE ENCODING OF "IS THIS BUFFER MINE?", READ BY BOTH RECORDS.**
//
// A String record and a buffer record carry the same
// problem: each one holds a `buffer@0` that may or may not be the record's own to write and to release,
// and every mutator and every drop path has to ask which. This file owns that question — the values
// that answer it and the emitters that read them — so the String runtime and the managed-memory runtime
// cannot answer it differently.
//
// ⚠ **THEY DID.** Array answered it with the SIGN of `capacity@16` while String answered it with
// `parent@32 == -1`, so the SAME FIELD at the SAME OFFSET meant OPPOSITE things in the two records: for
// an Array `parent@32` is *someone else's allocation that I owe a release to*, for a String it was *I
// own an external buffer*. One record's "this field is set" was the other's "this field is not". On top
// of that String carried a `capacity@16 = -2` that nothing ever tested and a `parent@32 = -3` that
// nothing ever read. Three spellings of one question across two runtimes, at one offset.
//
// --- THE ENCODING, stated once ---
//
// **`capacity@16` answers "IS THIS BUFFER MINE TO WRITE, AND MINE TO RELEASE?"**
//   * `>= 0` — YES. `buffer@0` is a SEPARATE allocation this record owns; it may be written in place
//     within `capacity` elements, and the record owes it a release when it dies or detaches.
//   * `< 0` — NO. The record must copy its bytes somewhere private before any in-place write, and must
//     not release `buffer@0`. WHICH negative value says only where the buffer came from (below); almost
//     no COMPILER-SIDE reader compares against a specific one, and that is deliberate — a further
//     non-owned-buffer kind then arrives as one more value rather than as a new test at every reader,
//     which is exactly how the zero-copy view arrived after the rdata literal.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THERE ARE EXACTLY THREE EXCEPTIONS, EACH WITH ONE HOME, AND A NEW PROVENANCE MUST BE TAUGHT
// ABOUT ALL THREE BY HAND.**
//
//   1. `emitRecordIsImmortal` — *"is this RECORD image data rather than an allocation?"*, asked by the
//      three readers that must not perform a refcount read-modify-write on a READ-ONLY `.rdata` page.
//   2. `emitBufferHostAllocation` (BATCH2 slice 1, moved here at W157) — *"which allocation must stay
//      alive for these bytes to be READABLE?"*, which is not *"which allocation do I owe a release to?"*:
//      the two part company for an INLINE String, whose bytes sit in its OWN payload and which therefore
//      owes nothing while its record is very much the allocation to count. "Inline" is not derivable from
//      the other slots (every non-owned provenance answers `NoOwedAllocation` at `parent@32`, and comparing
//      `buffer@0` against `record + StringRecordBytes` would misread an rdata blob the linker happened to
//      pack right after its record), so it tests the value.
//   3. `emitRouteOnVouchedTerminatorSlot` — *"may `buffer[length]` be READ at all?"*, the byte one PAST
//      the content, which `__mm_to_cstring` inspects to decide whether the receiver's bytes already ARE a
//      C string. It is the only one of the three that names MORE THAN ONE provenance, and the only one
//      whose wrong answer is an out-of-bounds READ rather than a miscount — see its own header.
//
// ⇒ **ADD A FIFTH `NotOwnedBufferCapacity` HERE AND NONE OF THE THREE WILL RECOGNIZE IT.** There is still
// no COMPILE error for that. All three are written so the UNTAUGHT answer is the conservative one — not
// immortal, hosted by nothing, and its terminator slot unreadable (so `__mm_to_cstring` copies) — which is
// a refused optimization or a missing count rather than a fault; but a missing count is a dangling view, so
// no residual is a substitute for teaching the value. This note is the obligation, written at the
// declaration because that is where the next value will be added. The runtime does NOT guard it with an
// abort arm, for this file's own stated reason: it refuses to emit a branch no input reaches (stated where
// `emitBufferCannotHold` stood, below).
//
// ✅ **W157 ADDED THE FOURTH (`ImmortalRecordCapacity`) AND PAID EXACTLY THIS TOLL**, which is why this
// note now says "fifth": the two readers that existed then were taught by hand in the same commit — and the
// THIRD reader (`emitRouteOnVouchedTerminatorSlot`) was written knowing all four, which is the only reason
// it could enumerate the three that vouch for their terminator slot and the one that cannot.
// ⛔ **AND ITS REVIEW MEASURED WHAT THE SHAPE OF THE TEACHING COSTS**: `emitBufferHostAllocation` was first taught as *"immortal ⇒
// nothing, OTHERWISE inline"*, and that residual is reached by an OWNER whose `buffer@0` is still 0 as well
// as by an inline String — exit 101 on a slice of an empty `ByteArray`. A provenance test must name the arm
// it is SURE of; the residual belongs to whatever the function can be wrong about safely.
//
// ⚠ **WHAT IS NO LONGER TRUE: "with nothing to catch it".** This note used to argue that the suite
// STRUCTURALLY could not reach the door, because `addressableBytes` is stdlib-only and the spec runner
// stages its fragments outside `stdlib/`. That was an argument about who may CALL it, and the callers that
// matter are inside `stdlib/`: `URL.maxon`, `helpers/url/urlHelpers.maxon` and `FilePath.maxon` were
// already loaded, and `File.maxon` added seven more call sites (`File.exists`, `readText`, `writeText`,
// `delete`, `rename`, `stat`, `FileReader.open`). Every stdlib module is parsed and compiled into EVERY
// program.
//
// ⭐ **MEASURED (N2), at RUN time and on both arms that exist to be told apart** — one program, an
// interpolated path (INLINE: `capacity@16 = InlineBufferCapacity`, bytes in the record's own payload) and a
// literal one (RDATA: the immortal blob) — `File.writeText` then `File.exists` then `File.readText` then
// `File.delete` on each: `built=true literal=true back=hello from 4`, exit 0, no leak. So a fourth
// provenance misclassified as inline would land as a wrong answer, a fault or a 101 on `file-io.md`,
// `file-info.md`, `filepath.md` and `url.md` — loudly, at the gate. That is the catcher this note said did
// not exist; it does not remove the obligation above, it says what ignoring it now costs.
//
// ⚠ **THE VALUES ARE NOT PRIVATE, THOUGH: `__managed_capacity` HANDS `capacity@16` STRAIGHT TO USER CODE**,
// so a Maxon program can read one, and `arrays.md:slice-capacity-is-a-view-until-written` pins
// `ViewBufferCapacity` at exactly `-1` — matching the reference compiler, which also answers `-1` for a
// buffer it does not yet own. So these four are RENUMBERABLE only in the sense that the suite will say
// so: change `ViewBufferCapacity` and that spec goes red. That is the right failure (loud, at the gate),
// but it is not "nothing observes the value".
//
// **`parent@32` answers "WHICH SEPARATE ALLOCATION DO I OWE A RELEASE TO?"** — the address `__mm_alloc`
// RETURNED (so `__mm_decref` finds its header just below it), or `NoOwedAllocation` for "none". It is
// consulted only when `capacity@16 < 0`; an owner's answer is its own `buffer@0`, and reading it out of
// two fields rather than storing it twice is what keeps `buffer` and `parent` from being one fact in
// two slots.
//
// ⭐ **THE FOUR PROVENANCES, and each is a record kind's answer to "where did these bytes come from?"**
// A view windows another allocation's bytes; an rdata literal points at an immortal `.rdata` blob whose
// record is nonetheless an ordinary heap box; an IMMORTAL RECORD is in `.rdata` along with its blob; an
// INLINE String's bytes sit inside the record's OWN `__mm_alloc` payload, at `record + StringRecordBytes`,
// so its own free reclaims them and there is no separate allocation to release. All four are simply
// NEGATIVE, which is the whole of what every reader inside the compiler asks BUT ONE — see the exception
// note above, which a further provenance must be read against — and otherwise the specific value is there
// for whoever reads a hexdump, or a `capacity()` a program printed (see the note above).
//
// ⚠ **`InlineBufferCapacity` IS WHY THE ENCODING NEEDED NO EXCEPTION FOR STRING.** An inline String's
// bytes are neither writable-in-place beyond their exact length nor separately releasable, which is
// precisely what a negative capacity says; it therefore takes the SAME two arms every other non-owned
// buffer takes. It briefly did not: the parser published `capacity@16 = length` (non-negative, so the
// encoding read the record as an owner of a separate buffer) and marked inline-ness in `parent@32`
// instead, which forced a String-only branch in front of `emitOwedAllocationBase` deriving inline-ness
// from the layout. The branch is gone, and so is the marker — this is the fact, in the field the rest of
// the encoding already lives in.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE TYPE IS WHAT MAKES "NOT MINE ⇒ NEGATIVE" A RULE RATHER THAN A HABIT.** Every provenance below
// is declared at this type, whose range STOPS at `-1`, so a fourth one written `= 4` — or `= 0` — does not
// compile. That matters because a GROWER need not test the sign at all: it can test
// `capacity < requiredLen` and rely on every non-owned capacity being below every byte count (the fused
// test `__str_append` used until W49 wave 8 — see the note where its emitter stood). Under a
// bare `ParsedInt` that reliance rested on three separate literals staying negative by convention, and a
// convention that is only written in a comment is a test nobody wrote — the exact shape of every bug this
// file exists to end. Here the compiler that builds shv2 checks it, once per provenance, at the
// declaration.
typealias NotOwnedBufferCapacity = int(i64.min to -1)

export let ViewBufferCapacity = -1 as NotOwnedBufferCapacity
export let RdataBufferCapacity = -2 as NotOwnedBufferCapacity
export let InlineBufferCapacity = -3 as NotOwnedBufferCapacity

// ⭐⭐ **THE FOURTH PROVENANCE (W157): THE BUFFER IS AN IMMORTAL `.rdata` BLOB *AND SO IS THE RECORD* — so
// nothing here may be increfed, decrefed or freed.** A String LITERAL is the only value born this way: its
// record is emitted into `.rdata` beside its blob (`StringRuntime.registerStringRecord`) and the PE writer
// maps that section READ-ONLY, so a refcount read-modify-write on it does not merely double-count, it
// FAULTS. A byte-string or constant-array literal keeps `RdataBufferCapacity`: its blob is immortal but its
// RECORD is an ordinary `__mm_alloc` box that `__managed_cow_detach` must be able to republish and
// `__managed_decref` must free, which is exactly the distinction the two values now carry.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT EXISTS BECAUSE W157 MADE AN IMMORTAL RECORD REACHABLE BY THE REFCOUNT PATHS FOR THE FIRST TIME.**
// Until then a literal's record was handed to no counting path at all — `__str_bytes_view` MINTED a heap
// view over its bytes, and the parser promotes a `var` String at its declaration, so `__str_decref` never
// saw one either. Now that `addressableBytes()` hands back the receiver's own record, a literal's record
// reaches `__mm_incref` and `__managed_decref`, and "which negative value" stops being a hexdump nicety.
// Its two readers are `StringRuntime.buildStrBytesView` (skip the incref) and
// `ManagedMemoryRuntime.__managed_decref` (skip the whole drop).
//
// ⭐ **THE OBLIGATION THE HEADER ABOVE RECORDS IS DISCHARGED FOR THIS VALUE, NOT INHERITED.** Both
// specific-provenance readers were taught it: `emitRecordIsImmortal` IS this value, and
// `emitBufferHostAllocation` answers "nothing hosts these bytes" for it — its blob outlives the process —
// through the residual it shares with the other two immortal-blob provenances. `__str_retain`'s
// copy-immortal arm moved from `RdataBufferCapacity` to this value for the same reason the record test did:
// it is the RECORD's writability that decides, not the blob's.
export let ImmortalRecordCapacity = -4 as NotOwnedBufferCapacity

// `parent@32` when the record owes NOTHING: an owner (its debt is its own `buffer@0`), a byte-string or
// String literal over an immortal `.rdata` blob, a String whose bytes are inline, or an array that never
// allocated a buffer at all. It is an ORDINARY answer, not an error case, which is why every release
// step is null-guarded.
export let NoOwedAllocation = 0 as ParsedInt

// --- ⭐⭐ THE BUFFER RECORD, DECLARED ONCE AND EMBEDDED WHOLE BY THE STRING RECORD ---
//
// **The String record and the Array record agree on ALL SIX slots, and `__mm_cow_detach` is what
// turned that agreement from a coincidence into a rule.** Until it existed, the emitters below took a
// `BufferRecordSlots` value that each runtime built from its own constants, and the header here said the
// two sets "coincide today and nothing here relies on that". Something does now: ONE emitted runtime
// function reads these offsets out of BOTH records, so a divergence would not be a second value flowing
// through a parameter — it would be a wrong load in a function that cannot tell which record it was
// handed. ⇒ the offsets are declared HERE, so the numbers exist once and the parameter that could only ever
// carry one value is gone. `StringRuntime` derives its own names from them (`StringBufferOffset`, …) because
// a String record is a DIFFERENT record and its spelling still says something; `ManagedMemoryRuntime` names
// these constants directly, its `Arr*Offset` aliases having retired with the file's own name (ARR6).
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE SIXTH SLOT JOINED THE PREFIX AT W157, AND THAT IS THE RUNG'S WHOLE THESIS.** `@40` used to be
// `singleByteGraphemes` in a String record and `element_destroy` in an Array record — genuinely different
// facts at one offset — so the shared prefix STOPPED at `@32`, and a String's bytes could reach buffer code
// only through a freshly minted 48-byte VIEW whose `@40` said `TrivialDestructor` where the String's said
// 0-or-1. A String record now carries a REAL `element_destroy@40`, always `TrivialElementDestroy`, which is
// not a placeholder but the truth — a String's elements are `Byte`s and need no per-element destructor —
// and its flag moved to a slot of its own at `@48`. So the six offsets below describe a WHOLE
// `__ManagedMemory`; a String record IS one, with one extra slot behind it; and `__managed_decref` and
// `emitRecordView` read all six out of both, which is the criterion this file states for what lives here.
export let ManagedRecordBufferOffset = 0 as ByteOffset
export let ManagedRecordLengthOffset = 8 as ByteOffset
export let ManagedRecordCapacityOffset = 16 as ByteOffset
export let ManagedRecordElementSizeOffset = 24 as ByteOffset
export let ManagedRecordParentOffset = 32 as ByteOffset

// The per-element destructor a managed-element array carries (P1.7 slice 3); for a TRIVIAL element it is
// `TrivialElementDestroy` and the drop path releases the buffer without walking. ⚠ **IT LIVED IN
// `ManagedMemoryRuntime` UNTIL W157**, under a header calling it *"the one slot that is genuinely THIS
// record's own"*. That stopped being true the moment a String record began carrying one and
// `__managed_decref` began reading it out of one, so it moved to where the other five already were.
export let ManagedRecordElementDestroyOffset = 40 as ByteOffset

// A BUFFER record is six 8-byte slots. Declared here for the same reason the offsets are: `__mm_cow_detach`
// is one function over both, and `LowerMaxonToStd` sizes the `__mm_alloc` of a literal's record from it.
// ⚠ A STRING record is this plus one slot; `StringRuntime.StringRecordBytes` DERIVES itself from this
// number rather than restating it, so the two cannot drift.
export let ManagedRecordBytes = 48 as BytePos

// `capacity < 0` — ⭐ **THE ONE HOME OF THE NOT-MINE TEST**, asked by the places that must act on it and
// reached by no other: `__managed_cow_detach` (such a buffer cannot be WRITTEN in place, so replace it),
// `__managed_decref` (it must not be released — it was either never allocated or is still being read by
// someone else) and `emitOwedAllocationBase` (which of two fields names the allocation this record owes
// a release to). Their conclusions differ and are meant to — what must never differ is the test, because
// a record misjudged by any one of them is a fault: a write into read-only memory or into bytes another
// array is reading, or a release of an address the allocator never handed out.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS ALSO THE ONE TEST THAT MAY BE ASKED BEFORE A HEADER READ IS SAFE**, and that is why it reads
// the record's own `capacity@16` and nothing else. A view's `buffer@0` is INTERIOR, so the word at
// `buffer - 8` is the parent's element DATA, not a refcount; anything that wants to read a buffer's
// `__mm_alloc` header must clear this test first. `__managed_cow_detach` is exactly such a reader, and its
// arms are ordered accordingly.
//
// ⚠ It is the ONLY buffer-provenance test left. `emitBufferCannotHold` below asked the same question
// WIDER — fused with "and is it big enough?" — and retired with its one caller at W49 wave 8; the note
// where it stood keeps the property that made the fusion sound, for a future grower to re-derive from.
export function emitBufferNotOwned(module StdModule, block IrBlock, record ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let capacity = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: record, offset: ManagedRecordCapacityOffset, ids: ids)
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids)
	return emitCmp(module, block: block, a: capacity, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
end 'emitBufferNotOwned'

// ⭐⭐ **REFUSE A BUFFER THIS RECORD DOES NOT OWN BEFORE AN OS CALL FILLS IT — THE ONE HOME OF THE
// WRITE-TARGET GUARD.** Asked by the two surfaces that hand a managed buffer to the KERNEL to be written
// into: `ManagedFileRuntime.__mf_read` and `ManagedSocketRuntime.__ms_recv`. It emits the test into `block`
// and routes the owned path to `fill`; `refuse` is the caller family's own throw block, because the two
// families have different error enums and the flag is not this function's to choose.
//
// ⚠ **`refuse` NEED NOT BE TERMINATED YET, AND THIS LINE USED TO CLAIM IT ALREADY WAS.** It is false at
// BOTH call sites — `__mf_read` terminates its `tooBig` block after this call, `__ms_recv` terminates its
// `failed` block through `emitSocketTransferOutcome` after this call — and it never mattered, because only
// `refuse.id` is read here. Stated as the requirement it actually is: an `IrBlock` this function may name
// as a branch target, whose contents are the caller's business and may be filled in afterwards.
//
// ⛔⛔ **NEITHER CALLER MAY DERIVE THE REFUSAL FROM ITS SIZE BOUND, AND BOTH ONCE DID — MEASURED, TWICE.**
// The tempting form is *"a non-owned record carries a NEGATIVE `capacity@16`, so any positive length is
// above it and the size check refuses it for free."* It does not. Every size bound at these two sites goes
// through `ManagedFileRuntime.emitArrayByteExtent` → `emitElementByteLen` → `emitBitsToBytes`, which ends in
// a LOGICAL shift right — so a `capacity` of `-1` comes back as `0x1FFFFFFFFFFFFFFF`: enormous, POSITIVE,
// and above every transfer either surface can be asked for. `__ms_send_from` accepted a 9-byte read out of
// a 4-byte window and sent it; `__mf_read` accepted a 24-byte `ReadFile` into a 4-byte zero-copy VIEW,
// answered `bytesRead = 24`, and rewrote the parent's four bytes plus 20 more past the end of its
// allocation. ⇒ the question has exactly ONE decoder — `emitBufferNotOwned` above — and a byte extent
// compared against anything is not it.
//
// ⚠ **WHAT IS EASY TO GET SILENTLY WRONG IS WHICH WAY ROUND THE `condBranch` GOES**, which is precisely the
// argument `ManagedFileRuntime.emitLiveHandleOrClosedThrow` makes for owning its family's other guard: swap
// the two edges in one copy and that surface stops guarding while its twin keeps working, so nothing goes
// red. One home, one direction.
//
// ⚠ **IT REFUSES RATHER THAN DETACHING, WHICH IS THE OPPOSITE OF WHAT EVERY `__managed_*` MUTATOR DOES**,
// and the asymmetry is real rather than an omission. A mutator writes bytes it already holds, so
// `emitCowDetach` can give the record a private copy and proceed; these two are handing a POINTER to the
// kernel and are bounded by a capacity, and a detach asks for exactly `length` elements
// (`buildManagedCowDetach`'s ask), so it buys the transfer no room the window did not already have and the
// capacity test would refuse the same calls one block later — after an allocation and a copy that a refused
// call must not pay for (`__managed_set_length`'s stated rule).
export function emitRefuseNonOwnedFillTarget(module StdModule, block IrBlock, record ValueId, refuse IrBlock, fill IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	let notWritable = emitBufferNotOwned(module, block: block, record: record, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.condBranch(notWritable, thenBlockId: refuse.id, elseBlockId: fill.id))
end 'emitRefuseNonOwnedFillTarget'

// `capacity == ImmortalRecordCapacity` — ⭐ **THE ONE HOME OF THE IS-THIS-RECORD-IMAGE-DATA TEST**
// (W157), `emitBufferNotOwned`'s twin and asked by the five places that must act on it:
// `StringRuntime.__str_bytes_view` (do not incref what cannot be written), `__str_retain` (copy it instead
// of co-owning it), `__str_decref` and `ManagedMemoryRuntime.__managed_decref` (do not count it and never
// free it) and `__managed_cursor_create` (do not retain it as a cursor's source).
// Their conclusions differ and are meant to; what must never differ is the test, because a record
// misjudged by any one of them is a refcount read-modify-write on a READ-ONLY `.rdata` page.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE LAST TWO ARE PAIRED WITH THE FIRST THREE, NOT INDEPENDENT OF THEM, AND THE PAIRING IS THE
// REVIEW'S WHOLE POINT (W157 review).** An acquire that skips must have a release that skips: `__str_bytes_view`
// hands an immortal record back uncounted and `__managed_decref` / `__str_decref` drop it untouched;
// `__managed_cursor_create` declines the retain and `__managed_cursor_decref` — which releases THROUGH
// `__managed_decref` — declines the release without a line of its own. ⛔ **A SKIP ADDED ON ONE SIDE ONLY IS
// A LEAK OR A DOUBLE FREE**, which is why every one of these reads THIS function rather than restating
// `== -4`, and why `__managed_cursor_decref` deliberately has no test of its own.
//
// ⚠ **`emitBufferHostAllocation` IS THE FOURTH READER OF THE PROVENANCE SPACE AND DELIBERATELY NOT A
// READER OF THIS.** It asks which allocation HOSTS the bytes, and an immortal record shares its answer
// ("none") with an rdata-blob literal, a view over one, and an owner that never allocated — so naming this
// value there would have been a test whose true arm was one of four cases needing the same answer, and
// whose false arm would then have had to mean "inline". It tests `InlineBufferCapacity` instead.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS NOT `emitBufferNotOwned` NARROWED, AND MUST NOT BE FOLDED INTO IT.** That one asks about the
// BUFFER — an inline String, a view and a byte-string literal all answer "not mine" while sitting in an
// ordinary `__mm_alloc` box that must be counted and freed. This asks about the RECORD, and exactly one
// provenance answers yes.
//
// ⚠ **`emitRouteOnVouchedTerminatorSlot` NAMES `ImmortalRecordCapacity` TOO AND IS NOT A SIXTH READER OF
// THIS TEST**, which is why it is a separate exception in the header rather than a line added above. It
// asks a different question — *"may the byte AFTER the buffer be read?"* — whose yes-set is three
// provenances, not one; it therefore has no paired release to keep in step, and it holds the capacity
// already. What the two DO share is the comparison itself, and they share it as code: both go through
// `emitCapacityIsSentinel` below, so "compare `capacity@16` against a named provenance" still has one
// spelling.
export function emitRecordIsImmortal(module StdModule, block IrBlock, record ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let capacity = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: record, offset: ManagedRecordCapacityOffset, ids: ids)
	return emitCapacityIsSentinel(module, block: block, capacity: capacity, sentinel: ImmortalRecordCapacity, ids: ids)
end 'emitRecordIsImmortal'

// `capacity@16 == <one named provenance>` — the two-op spelling every SPECIFIC-provenance test in this file
// is made of, taking the capacity ALREADY LOADED rather than the record. All THREE specific-provenance
// readers go through it — `emitRecordIsImmortal` above, `emitBufferHostAllocation` below, and the
// terminator-slot router, which alone asks it three times over one load. Written inline it would have been
// this body spelled out SIX times, which is the one duplication this file exists to prevent, in the file
// whose whole subject is that the provenance test has ONE spelling.
//
// ⚠ The parameter is `NotOwnedBufferCapacity`, so a caller cannot pass a byte count: this comparison is
// only ever against a declared provenance, and asking it about `0` or `length` is a different question with
// a different emitter (`emitBufferNotOwned`, or the `>` in the router).
function emitCapacityIsSentinel(module StdModule, block IrBlock, capacity ValueId, sentinel NotOwnedBufferCapacity, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let value = emitConst(module, block: block, value: sentinel as ParsedInt, ids: ids)
	return emitCmp(module, block: block, a: capacity, b: value, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
end 'emitCapacityIsSentinel'

// ⭐⭐ **THE THIRD EXCEPTION: "MAY `buffer[length]` BE READ AT ALL?" — the ONE HOME of the terminator-slot
// test, and its absence was a LIVE out-of-bounds read rather than a theoretical hazard.**
//
// `buffer[length]` is one byte PAST the content. `__mm_to_cstring` — the whole of `String.cstr()`, which is
// PUBLIC and which an ordinary user program reaches — inspects it to decide whether the receiver's own
// bytes already ARE a C string and can be handed back with nothing allocated. That inspection is only
// legitimate for a record that can vouch for the byte, and this routes on exactly that: `vouched` takes the
// records that can, `unvouched` the records that cannot and must therefore be GROWN by one byte and
// terminated in place instead (`StringRuntime.buildMmToCString`'s second path).
//
// ⭐ **THE FOUR SHAPES THAT CAN VOUCH, each for its own reason, and they are an ADMIT-LIST for the reason
// this file's header states — an untaught FIFTH provenance must land on the copy path, not on the probe.**
//
//   * an OWNED buffer with a SPARE SLOT (`capacity > length`) — the slot is INSIDE the record's own
//     allocation, which is the whole of what is claimed here. (A freshly grown buffer's `[length, capacity)`
//     also reads zero — `buildManagedGrow`'s zeroing contract — so the probe usually SUCCEEDS as well; a
//     buffer that has since been shortened may hold a stale non-zero byte there and simply routes to the
//     grow. Either answer is sound, because either byte is the record's own to read.)
//   * `RdataBufferCapacity` — a byte-string / constant-array literal's blob, in the IMMUTABLE image. Its
//     following byte is whatever the `.rdata` packer put there (`registerByteArrayLiteralBlob` appends no
//     NUL, unlike `stringBlobBytes`); that is a byte no allocator ever hands out and nothing can ever write,
//     so a zero found there is a terminator that STAYS one, and a non-zero one simply routes to the grow.
//     ⚠ **NO INPUT REACHES THIS ARM TODAY** — the corpus's one `toCString()` call is `String.cstr()`'s, so
//     the only route to an rdata BUFFER would be an `arr.managed.toCString()` a `stdlib/` module does not
//     write. It is written anyway, and that is not this file's "refuses to emit a branch no input reaches"
//     rule being broken: that rule is about a defensive ABORT for a provenance nobody taught, whereas this
//     is one clause of a RULE whose other half lives in the C# bootstrap — where the same shape IS reached,
//     by every string literal. An admit-list that silently dropped a shape it knows to be safe would leave
//     the two compilers holding different rules, which is the whole thing this pair exists to avoid.
//   * `InlineBufferCapacity` — a fused String's bytes live in its own `__mm_alloc` payload, which
//     `emitAllocOwnedInlineString` sizes `length + StringRecordBytes + 1` (`StringInterpOverheadBytes`)
//     precisely so the terminator slot is the record's own; the slab's zeroing contract supplies the zero.
//   * `ImmortalRecordCapacity` — a String LITERAL, whose blob `StringRuntime.stringBlobBytes` NUL-terminates
//     inside `.rdata`. Same immutability argument as the rdata blob, plus a guaranteed zero.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE TWO IT REFUSES, AND WHY NEITHER IS AN EDGE CASE.**
//
//   * a VIEW (`ViewBufferCapacity`) — its bytes are a window into someone else's buffer, so the byte after
//     the window is not the view's, and when the window reaches its parent's end it is not the parent's
//     either. ⚠ `Parser.requireStdlibOnlyBufferMethod` records an exit-101 MEASURED on a view here, and it
//     measured the OTHER defect: the copy that path used to make leaked. That copy is gone (the record is
//     grown in place now), so the number no longer reproduces — what the view is refused for is the READ,
//     which no exit code has ever shown.
//   * an EXACTLY-FULL OWNED buffer (`capacity == length`) — the ORDINARY state, not a corner.
//     `__managed_grown_cap` takes an ask that outruns doubling at face value, and a first append onto a
//     non-owned buffer always does (`doubled` is negative), so `var s = ""` + `s.append("hello world")`
//     leaves `capacity == length` on the very first append. The byte belongs to whatever the allocator
//     hands out next, and nothing keeps it zero.
//
// ⚠ **AN UNVOUCHED RECORD IS THEREFORE ONE THAT WILL BE WRITTEN, WHICH IS A SECOND OBLIGATION ON THIS
// ADMIT-LIST AND POINTS THE OPPOSITE WAY.** Everything in the `unvouched` set — a view, an exactly-full
// owner, any untaught fifth provenance — is an ordinary `__mm_alloc` box whose header the COW may republish.
// The one record in this language that is NOT writable is `ImmortalRecordCapacity`'s, which lives in
// read-only `.rdata`; it is in the VOUCHED set, and its blob is NUL-terminated by `stringBlobBytes`, so it
// takes the probe and the probe always says yes. ⇒ **A future provenance whose RECORD is read-only may not
// simply be left off the admit-list**, the way the conservative default otherwise allows: leaving it off
// routes it to the write. See `buildMmToCString`'s header, which states the same pairing from the caller's
// side.
//
// ⭐ **MEASURED IN THE C# BOOTSTRAP, WHOSE `MaxonToStandardConversion.LowerManagedToCString` CARRIES THE
// SAME RULE AND MUST MOVE WITH THIS ONE.** There a 48-byte relative path answered `Directory.exists` =
// **false** for a directory plainly present: `maxon_directory_exists` reaches `__io_submit_sync`, whose
// `mm_raw_alloc` landed on the byte the probe had just accepted as that path's terminator. ⚠ **THE
// DUPLICATION IS ACKNOWLEDGED AND IRREDUCIBLE**: two compilers in two languages emitting two IRs cannot
// share the code, so they share the RULE and each names the other. shv2's admit-list is LONGER than the
// bootstrap's — four shapes against two — because shv2's provenance space is: it splits the bootstrap's
// single rdata sentinel into `RdataBufferCapacity` / `ImmortalRecordCapacity` and adds
// `InlineBufferCapacity`, which is the shape almost every heap String in this language actually has.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS A ROUTER RATHER THAN A `returns ValueId`, and that is the property that makes it a bounds
// check.** The probe LOAD must sit in a block only the admitting arms reach — a materialized boolean would
// let the load be emitted beside it in `cur`, which is the out-of-bounds read this exists to remove.
// `emitRefuseNonOwnedFillTarget` above is the same shape for the same reason.
//
// ⚠ **THE `>` IS SIGNED** (`emitCmp` defaults to `StdType.i64` and `stdTypeIsUnsigned` is false for it), so
// every negative provenance fails `capacity > length` and can only reach `vouched` through the sentinel
// test that names it. ⛔ **DO NOT COLLAPSE THE THREE SENTINEL ARMS INTO `capacity < ViewBufferCapacity`.**
// The three safe values happen to be contiguous below `-1` today; an ordering test over that would ADMIT a
// fifth provenance sight unseen, which is the one direction this file's header forbids failing in.
export function emitRouteOnVouchedTerminatorSlot(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, record ValueId, length ValueId, vouched IrBlock, unvouched IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	let capacity = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: record, offset: ManagedRecordCapacityOffset, ids: ids)

	// `cur` dominates all three, so the one `capacity` load serves every arm — the same dominance argument
	// `ManagedFileRuntime.emitPathToCString` makes for its own `len`.
	var checkRdata = module.addBlock(func, label: b"vouchrdata")
	var checkInline = module.addBlock(func, label: b"vouchinline")
	var checkImmortal = module.addBlock(func, label: b"vouchimmortal")

	let hasSpareSlot = emitCmp(module, block: cur, a: capacity, b: length, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(hasSpareSlot, thenBlockId: vouched.id, elseBlockId: checkRdata.id))

	emitRouteOnCapacitySentinel(module, block: checkRdata, capacity: capacity, sentinel: RdataBufferCapacity, whenIt: vouched, otherwise: checkInline, ids: ids)
	emitRouteOnCapacitySentinel(module, block: checkInline, capacity: capacity, sentinel: InlineBufferCapacity, whenIt: vouched, otherwise: checkImmortal, ids: ids)
	emitRouteOnCapacitySentinel(module, block: checkImmortal, capacity: capacity, sentinel: ImmortalRecordCapacity, whenIt: vouched, otherwise: unvouched, ids: ids)
end 'emitRouteOnVouchedTerminatorSlot'

// One link of the admit chain above: terminate `block` with "is the capacity THIS provenance?", taking the
// admitting edge or handing the question on. Written once because the three links differ in exactly one
// value, and three copies of a three-line stanza is how a fourth link would come to be added to two of them.
function emitRouteOnCapacitySentinel(module StdModule, block IrBlock, capacity ValueId, sentinel NotOwnedBufferCapacity, whenIt IrBlock, otherwise IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	let isSentinel = emitCapacityIsSentinel(module, block: block, capacity: capacity, sentinel: sentinel, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.condBranch(isSentinel, thenBlockId: whenIt.id, elseBlockId: otherwise.id))
end 'emitRouteOnCapacitySentinel'

// ⚠⚠ **`emitBufferCannotHold` STOOD HERE AND WENT WITH ITS ONE CALLER (W49 wave 8).** It emitted
// `capacity@16 < requiredLen` — the ONE place a caller was allowed to FUSE "is this buffer mine?" with
// "is it big enough?" — and its only caller was `StringRuntime.__str_append`, which retired with
// `String.append`. A zero-caller emitter is deleted rather than kept warm: this file's own rule is that
// vocabulary does not ship ahead of its consumer.
//
// ⭐ **THE PROPERTY IT RESTED ON IS NOT DELETED, BECAUSE IT IS THE TYPE'S AND NOT THE HELPER'S**, and the
// next grower written against this encoding needs it:
//
//   a byte COUNT is `>= 0`; `NotOwnedBufferCapacity` STOPS at `-1`, so every non-owned capacity is `< 0`.
//   Every negative is below every non-negative, so `capacity < requiredLen` is UNCONDITIONALLY true for
//   an inline String, for a literal's immortal `.rdata` blob, for a view, and for any provenance added
//   later — which cannot opt out, because it cannot be declared non-negative.
//
// ⇒ A future fused test may be re-derived from that sentence in one line. What it may NOT do is pair it
// with `emitBufferNotOwned` in an `OR`: an `OR` whose left arm can never be the deciding one is a branch
// no input reaches, and this file refuses to emit those.

// ⭐ **THE SEPARATE ALLOCATION THIS RECORD OWES A RELEASE TO — `NoOwedAllocation` when it owes nothing.**
// An OWNER's is its own `buffer@0` (the pointer `__mm_alloc` returned, which may be 0 if the record never
// allocated one); a NON-OWNED record's is `parent@32` (a view's shared base, or 0 for an immortal `.rdata`
// blob). ⭐ THE ONE HOME OF THAT CHOICE, read by every place whose correctness depends on picking the same
// field: `__managed_slice` (which allocation to INCREF for the new view), `__mm_cow_detach` (which one to
// RELEASE once the record has copied its bytes out — it reads the base here and RETURNS it, and both
// detaching callers reach this through that one function rather than each asking) and
// `__managed_decref`/`__str_decref` (which one to release at death). A disagreement between any two of them
// is a double-free or a leak, so it is written once.
//
// ⭐ **TWO ARMS COVER EVERY RECORD KIND, AND NOTHING WRAPS THIS WITH A THIRD.** An owner's buffer is
// always a separate allocation, because the only bytes that are NOT — an inline String's — are marked by
// a negative `capacity@16` like every other non-owned buffer, so they leave through the `parent@32` arm
// (`NoOwedAllocation`) with no special case here and none at the caller.
//
// It threads `cur` (sets its terminator) and returns the base as a BLOCK ARG on the caller-owned `join`,
// which is why `join` is passed IN: the caller resumes there and the two arms are otherwise identical.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT FUSED WITH `emitReleaseOwedBase` INTO ONE "release the base" HELPER**, even
// though the drop paths call the two back to back. A DETACH must SPLIT them across its byte copy — the
// base is read before the record is republished and spent only after the copy — so a fused helper would
// serve some call sites and quietly invite the ones that must not fuse them to do so.
export function emitOwedAllocationBase(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, join IrBlock, record ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	var viewBase = module.addBlock(func, label: b"vbase")
	var ownBase = module.addBlock(func, label: b"obase")

	let notOwned = emitBufferNotOwned(module, block: cur, record: record, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(notOwned, thenBlockId: viewBase.id, elseBlockId: ownBase.id))

	let base = ids.mint()
	join.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(base, argType: StdType.i64))

	let parent = emitLoadField(module, block: viewBase, record: record, offset: ManagedRecordParentOffset, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(viewBase, op: StdOp.branch(join.id))
	viewBase.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(join.id, argIds: argsOf(parent)))

	let buffer = emitLoadField(module, block: ownBase, record: record, offset: ManagedRecordBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(ownBase, op: StdOp.branch(join.id))
	ownBase.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(join.id, argIds: argsOf(buffer)))

	return base
end 'emitOwedAllocationBase'

// `if base != NoOwedAllocation then <callee>(base)` — the null-guarded refcount step every share and
// every release of an owed allocation makes, since "this record owes nothing" is an ordinary answer from
// `emitOwedAllocationBase`. Threads `cur` and returns the continuation block. ONE home for the guard, in
// both directions: `__managed_slice` raises the count with `__mm_incref`, the detach and drop paths lower it
// with `__mm_decref`.
//
// ⭐ **A BUFFER IS RELEASED WITH `__mm_decref`, NEVER `__mm_free`, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE OF THE DROP
// RULE.** It may still be under a view, so only the LAST owner may reclaim it — which is exactly what
// `__mm_decref` decides. It is also the safer failure mode: if some future path ever reached here with a
// count it should not have, a decref merely keeps the buffer alive and the leak gate reports 101, where
// a `__mm_free` would silently hand a live allocation back and become a use-after-free the day a free
// list exists. v1 paid for the twin of this omission three times
// (`stdlib/Internals.maxon:3465-3472`): a new non-owned-buffer kind must be taught to the DROP path, not
// only to the write check.
export function emitReleaseOwedBase(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, base ValueId, callee ByteArray, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var step = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rcstep")
	let cont = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rccont")

	let zero = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: NoOwedAllocation, ids: ids)
	let shares = emitCmp(module, block: cur, a: base, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(shares, thenBlockId: step.id, elseBlockId: cont.id))

	let counted = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(step, op: StdOp.call(counted, callee: callee, args: argsOf(base)))
	module.setTerminator(step, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))

	return cont
end 'emitReleaseOwedBase'

// ⭐⭐ **WHICH ALLOCATION MUST STAY ALIVE FOR THIS RECORD'S BUFFER TO BE READABLE — `NoOwedAllocation` when
// none must.** A near-twin of `emitOwedAllocationBase`, and DELIBERATELY BUILT ON IT rather than beside it,
// because the two questions differ by exactly one provenance and the rest of the answer must not be
// restated: *"which separate allocation do I owe a release to?"* and *"where do these bytes live?"* agree
// for an OWNER (its own `buffer@0`), for a VIEW (its `parent@32`) and for an immortal literal (nothing) —
// and part company for an INLINE String, whose bytes sit inside its OWN `__mm_alloc` payload. That one
// honestly owes NOTHING, which is the right answer to the first question and a dangling `buffer@0` as an
// answer to this one.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT IS THE QUESTION A VIEW MUST ASK, AND `emitRecordView` ASKED THE OTHER ONE UNTIL W157.** That was
// invisible while no INLINE record could be sliced — an Array's elements are never inside its own record,
// so for an Array the two answers coincide at every provenance. `String.addressableBytes()` now hands its
// receiver's own record to the buffer surface, so `path.addressableBytes().slice(…)` slices an INLINE
// record: the owed-base answer is `NoOwedAllocation`, and a view built on it would hold a raw interior
// pointer into a String's payload with no count on it. Asking THIS question instead counts the record.
//
// ⚠ **IT LIVED IN `StringRuntime` AS `emitStringBytesAllocation`.** It was a String's question only for as
// long as a String record was not a buffer record; it is the shared one now, and it sits beside the
// question it is built on.
//
// So: ask the shared question first, and answer this one only where it says "nothing".
//
//   * a non-zero owed base — an OWNER's own buffer, or a VIEW's shared base. Both are the allocation the
//     elements are in, and a view's flattens exactly as `emitRecordView`'s nesting note describes, so no
//     chain is built or walked.
//   * `capacity@16 == InlineBufferCapacity` — an INLINE String, whose bytes sit at
//     `record + StringRecordBytes` inside the record's OWN `__mm_alloc` payload, so the RECORD is the
//     allocation that must stay alive. This is the one place a specific provenance value has to be compared
//     against, because "inline" is not derivable from the other slots: every non-owned provenance answers
//     `NoOwedAllocation` at `parent@32`, and comparing `buffer@0` against `record + StringRecordBytes` would
//     misread an rdata blob the linker happened to pack right after its record.
//     ⚠ **THAT COMPARISON IS THE EXCEPTION TO THIS FILE'S STATED RULE** ("almost no compiler-side reader
//     compares against a specific one"), so the OBLIGATION it creates is recorded at the DECLARATION of
//     the provenances, where the next one will be added — not only here, where nobody adding one would look.
//   * otherwise — NOTHING hosts these bytes, and the records that land here say so for three different
//     reasons: an immortal `.rdata` blob outlives the process, a view over one windows that same blob, and an
//     OWNER that never allocated a buffer has no bytes at all.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE RESIDUAL ARM ANSWERS `NoOwedAllocation`, AND ANSWERING "THE RECORD" THERE WAS A LEAK —
// MEASURED, exit 101, found in W157's own review.** The arm was first written as *"immortal ⇒ nothing,
// otherwise ⇒ the record"*, and that `otherwise` is reached by an OWNER whose `buffer@0` is still 0 just as
// much as by an inline String. So `slice(0, endIndex: 0)` of a fresh `ByteArray.create()` put a count on the
// ARRAY's record; the array then grew a buffer, and its `__managed_decref` — no longer the last owner —
// lowered the count and returned WITHOUT releasing that buffer, which the view's later `__mm_decref` /
// `__mm_free` of the record structurally cannot reach. ⇒ **the arm that names the record must be the
// POSITIVE test**, so a provenance nobody taught this function about is refused a count rather than handed
// one it cannot honour.
//
// ⭐ **THE COUNT IS CORRECT IN BOTH DROP ORDERS, in every arm.** DETACHED: the view increfs `buffer@0` and
// the record's own drop decrefs the same address on its last-owner path, so whichever dies second frees it.
// INLINE: the view increfs the RECORD, so the record's drop finds a non-zero count, lowers it and keeps the
// record — and the view's `__managed_decref` then `__mm_decref`s it, which for an inline String is exactly
// right because it owes nothing else. NOTHING-HOSTS-IT: nothing happens, twice.
export function emitBufferHostAllocation(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, join IrBlock, record ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	var owedJoin = module.addBlock(func, label: b"hostowed")
	var separate = module.addBlock(func, label: b"hostsep")
	var noSeparate = module.addBlock(func, label: b"hostnosep")
	var inlineBytes = module.addBlock(func, label: b"hostinline")
	var nothingHosts = module.addBlock(func, label: b"hostnone")

	let owedBase = emitOwedAllocationBase(module, func: func, cur: cur, join: owedJoin, record: record, ids: ids)

	let base = ids.mint()
	join.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(base, argType: StdType.i64))

	let zero = emitConst(module, block: owedJoin, value: NoOwedAllocation, ids: ids)
	let owesOne = emitCmp(module, block: owedJoin, a: owedBase, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(owedJoin, op: StdOp.condBranch(owesOne, thenBlockId: separate.id, elseBlockId: noSeparate.id))

	module.setTerminator(separate, op: StdOp.branch(join.id))
	separate.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(join.id, argIds: argsOf(owedBase)))

	// The ONE positive provenance test — the header states why the residual may not answer `record`. It goes
	// through the shared link for the reason `emitCapacityIsSentinel` states: this file's own subject is that
	// "compare `capacity@16` against a named provenance" has ONE spelling, so it may not have three.
	let capacity = emitLoadField(module, block: noSeparate, record: record, offset: ManagedRecordCapacityOffset, ids: ids)
	emitRouteOnCapacitySentinel(module, block: noSeparate, capacity: capacity, sentinel: InlineBufferCapacity, whenIt: inlineBytes, otherwise: nothingHosts, ids: ids)

	module.setTerminator(inlineBytes, op: StdOp.branch(join.id))
	inlineBytes.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(join.id, argIds: argsOf(record)))

	let nothing = emitConst(module, block: nothingHosts, value: NoOwedAllocation, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(nothingHosts, op: StdOp.branch(join.id))
	nothingHosts.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(join.id, argIds: argsOf(nothing)))

	return base
end 'emitBufferHostAllocation'

// --- ⭐⭐ `__mm_cow_detach(record, askElements) -> owedBase` — THE COPY-ON-WRITE ACTION, ONCE ---
//
// **Give this record a PRIVATE buffer of `askElements` elements holding its live ones, and hand back the
// allocation it used to owe a release to.** It is the ACTION half of copy-on-write; the QUESTION half stays
// exactly where it already was (`emitBufferNotOwned` above), and each caller goes
// on asking its own — their conditions genuinely differ and are not this function's business.
//
// ⚠ **THE QUESTION WAS UNIFIED HERE LONG BEFORE THE ACTION WAS, AND THAT GAP IS WHAT THIS CLOSES.** This
// file's header records that Array and String once answered *"is this buffer mine?"* with different fields
// at the same offset, and that the cure was to own the question here. The ACTION stayed forked anyway:
// `__managed_cow_detach` was Array's, and String open-coded its own detach inline inside `__str_append`. Two
// spellings of *"copy my bytes somewhere private and republish"* — the same defect one level up, and a
// `__str_cow_detach` beside `__managed_cow_detach` would have made it permanent.
//
// It is `__mm_`-prefixed because it is a MANAGED-MEMORY operation over the shared record shape rather than
// either container's: both runtimes CALL it, neither owns it. ⭐ The prefix also earns its `usesHeap` bit
// for free (`MmRuntime.isRuntimeCallee`). It is BUILT here rather than in `MmRuntime` because it reads five
// record fields, and `MmRuntime` deliberately knows only the allocator HEADER's geometry — its own note
// says that geometry "is private to this file, and stays that way". Its NAME lives with the other `__mm_*`
// names there, where the one list of them is.
//
// ⭐ **THE ASK IS IN ELEMENTS, NOT BYTES, AND THAT IS WHAT LETS ONE FUNCTION SERVE BOTH RECORDS.** Every
// byte count here goes through `emitElementByteLen` against the record's OWN `element_size@24` — so a
// String (always 1) gets `askElements` bytes, an `Array with <a wide element>` gets `askElements · es`, and
// a PACKED array gets `ceil(askElements · bits / 8)`. `capacity@16` is published as `askElements`, the unit
// both records already keep it in. A detach that assumed a stride of 1 would be right for every String and
// would under-allocate every wide-element array by exactly its stride.
//
// ⭐ **GROWTH POLICY STAYS WITH THE CALLER, DELIBERATELY.** `__managed_cow_detach` — the one caller left — asks
// for exactly `length`, because slack is `__managed_grow`/`__managed_grown_cap`'s separate business, and a detach
// that also grew would give one record two growth policies. The ask is therefore a PARAMETER rather than a
// rule written here.
//
// ⚠ **`__str_append` WAS THE SECOND CALLER AND FUSED THE TWO, ASKING `2 * requiredLen`; IT RETIRED WITH
// `String.append` (W49 wave 8) AND ITS REASONING DID NOT.** That doubling was never a preference: exact
// growth made `var s = ""` plus a loop of appends QUADRATIC (measured ×3.94/×4.07/×3.82/×3.88 per doubling,
// and 5.0 s to build a 288 KB string). The corpus body appends through `managed.append(other.managed)` now,
// so the geometric growth a String depends on is `__managed_grown_cap`'s — the same policy, reached one layer
// out. ⇒ **A caller of this function that neither grows geometrically itself nor routes through
// `__managed_grow` restores that quadratic**, and it restores it silently, because a detach is correct however
// small the ask.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT RETURNS THE OWED BASE INSTEAD OF RELEASING IT, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING RATHER THAN TIDY.**
// `emitOwedAllocationBase` and `emitReleaseOwedBase` are deliberately unfused (see their headers) because a
// detach must SPLIT them across its byte copy, and the far side of that split is the CALLER's to name.
// `__managed_cow_detach` may pay the debt the instant this returns. `__str_append` may NOT, which is why the
// split exists at all: `s.append(s)` aliased its `other` operand to `self`'s OLD buffer, so the release had
// to wait until the BLIT had also finished reading, one block later. Releasing inside here would
// reintroduce exactly the read-after-free that `string-type-2/rc-repeated-self-append` exists to guard —
// and, per that case's own note, NO TEST COULD CATCH IT TODAY, because `__mm_free` reclaims nothing under
// the bump allocator. The debt therefore still comes back out as a return value, and the caller pays it
// when its own reads are done.
//
// ⭐ **`s.append(s)` IS STILL SAFE ON THE CORPUS ROUTE, AND BY A DIFFERENT MECHANISM — TRACED, NOT ASSUMED.**
// `__managed_append` loads BOTH buffers *after* its `__managed_reserve`, and `__managed_grow` detaches and reallocates
// BEFORE returning, copying the live bytes into the new allocation and releasing the old one there. So the
// aliased read lands in the NEW buffer, which already holds the original contents, and the two ranges do
// not overlap (`[0, n)` read into `[n, 2n)` written). `__str_append` needed the split because it blitted
// out of the buffer the detach had just released; this route never reads the released one at all.
//
// The traps `buildManagedCowDetach`'s header lists are kept here rather than restated there: the old buffer is
// loaded AFTER the owed base is read, a ZERO-LENGTH record still detaches (no early-out — `__mm_alloc(0, 0)`
// is a legal empty box, and skipping would leave a sentinel capacity on a pointer that has no header for the
// next reallocation to read), and the fresh buffer comes from `__mm_alloc`, which zeroes.
export function buildMmCowDetach(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(MmCowDetachName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var baseJoin = module.addBlock(func, label: b"basejoin")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let record = 0 as ValueId
	let askElements = 1 as ValueId

	// entry: read the allocation this record owes a release to BEFORE anything is republished — the answer is
	// derived from the very `capacity`/`buffer`/`parent` fields the publish below overwrites.
	let base = emitOwedAllocationBase(module, func: func, cur: entry, join: baseJoin, record: record, ids: ids)

	// baseJoin: size both the ask and the live extent through the record's own stride, allocate, copy across.
	let length = emitLoadField(module, block: baseJoin, record: record, offset: ManagedRecordLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let es = emitLoadField(module, block: baseJoin, record: record, offset: ManagedRecordElementSizeOffset, ids: ids)
	let liveBytes = emitElementByteLen(module, block: baseJoin, count: length, es: es, ids: ids)
	let askBytes = emitElementByteLen(module, block: baseJoin, count: askElements, es: es, ids: ids)
	let bufferDtor = emitConst(module, block: baseJoin, value: TrivialElementDestroy, ids: ids)
	let newBuf = ids.mint()
	emitMmAllocCall(module, block: baseJoin, result: newBuf, size: askBytes, destructor: bufferDtor, tag: mmTagIndex(MmTag.ElementBuffer), ids: ids)
	let oldBuf = emitLoadField(module, block: baseJoin, record: record, offset: ManagedRecordBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	var afterCopy = emitForwardCopy(module, func: func, cur: baseJoin, dest: newBuf, src: oldBuf, byteCount: liveBytes, ids: ids)

	// afterCopy: publish the private buffer. `parent@32` returns to `NoOwedAllocation` — a record that has
	// just copied its bytes into an allocation of its own owes nothing to anyone else.
	emitStoreField(module, block: afterCopy, record: record, offset: ManagedRecordBufferOffset, value: newBuf)
	emitStoreField(module, block: afterCopy, record: record, offset: ManagedRecordCapacityOffset, value: askElements)
	let noParent = emitConst(module, block: afterCopy, value: NoOwedAllocation, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: afterCopy, record: record, offset: ManagedRecordParentOffset, value: noParent)
	module.setTerminator(afterCopy, op: StdOp.ret(base))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildMmCowDetach'
